// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(5): "A destructor,
// allocation function, or deallocation function shall not be declared
// `consteval`." An ordinary member function is deliberately *not* in that
// list, so a `static` member function may be declared `consteval` -- which
// matches [dcl.constexpr]/1-2 unchanged, adopted here via §9.1(1)/(2) and
// front-matter §1(2).
// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(4) then requires the
// call to produce a constant expression, and
// docs/spec/en/06-constant-evaluation.md (ch06) §7.2(6.2) requires the
// implementation to support it.
//
// Call position: block scope. Callee: `consteval` static member function.
// There is no receiver -- a static member function is called on the class,
// not on an object.
//
// Expected value derived from the spec text above and independently confirmed
// with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which compiles this exact source and exits 42.
class Units {
public:
    virtual ~Units() = default;
public:
    static consteval int scaled(int x) {
        return x * 2;
    }
};

int main() {
    return Units::scaled(21);
}
